# Meltdown

## Overview

Meltdown is an open-field tower-defense game for the browser, played on the floor
of a reactor. Waves of surge intruders pour in through vents cut into the casing
wall and cross the floor toward the exhausts, and the player stops them by
building emitter towers on the open floor.

Every tower is also a wall, so there is no fixed path. The player builds the maze
the surge walks, winding it the long way round so the emitters have time to burn
it down.

Meltdown's defining idea is heat as power. An emitter fires harder the hotter it
runs, climbing to full power at its own redline and holding it there, and an
emitter carried all the way to the top of the scale trips offline to cool,
leaving a hole in the defense. A tower sheds heat only through the faces that
touch open air, so a tight block of guns bakes its own core, and each tower's
radiator faces are aimed by rotating it before it is placed. Two structures
sculpt the heat, a thermostatic Forge that warms its neighbours toward a setpoint
and a Sink that draws heat out through a face nothing else can cool through, and
the cryo Rime runs the rule backward and slows hardest when it is cold.

Meltdown has its own name, look, and rules, and its branding and design are
original to it.

The specification spans several files under `specs/`. `specs/floor.md` gives the
reactor floor's geometry and the tile-to-stage map; `specs/mazing.md` how towers
wall the floor and how the surge crosses it; `specs/heat.md` the heat model, the
damage curve, and the trip; `specs/towers.md` the eight towers as data;
`specs/combat.md` what an emitter does to a surge unit; `specs/building.md` what
a player does to a tower; `specs/surge.md` the six intruder types;
`specs/waves.md` the run of phases and waves; `specs/economy.md` money and score;
`specs/modes.md` the five modes and the three difficulties;
`specs/controls.md` the pointer and the keys; `specs/hud.md` the build panel and
the on-floor reads; `specs/screens.md` the eight screens and their menus;
`specs/audio.md` the cues and mute; `specs/state.md` what the game's state
carries; `specs/instrumentation.md` the debug and automation surface; and
`specs/showcase.md` the showcase the finished build ships. Read all of them and
build the game they describe as one cohesive whole.

