### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served as-is
from any static file server, at any base path. The scripts that do this are
already in `package.json`.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser. Aim
for a game a person would enjoy playing: tense, readable, and deliberate.

The build ships the whole game: the walled floor and its four openings, the eight
towers, the heat model and the trip, the six surge types, the wave progression
and its economy, the five modes, the eight screens, and the build panel. Every
other spec file states one part of it.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio, and the debug overlay.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the input actions,
  the pointer, audio, and the debug overlay from it.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, rendering, the
  input actions, the pointer, audio, and the debug overlay from it, and writing
  the game inside its framework of game modes, actors, components, and
  controllers.
{{/if}}
- Render real graphics on the canvas. A text-only or ASCII rendering does not
  satisfy this requirement.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is
  self-contained, and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `MeltdownState`, as `specs/state.md` declares
  it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
- Hold the game's whole authoritative state in `MeltdownState`, the world's game
  state, as `specs/state.md` declares it.
{{/if}}
- Play with the pointer and with a touchscreen. Every action and every menu is
  reachable by pointer alone, with the keys of `specs/controls.md` alongside it.
- Expose the debug and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` specifies,
  and the read-only debug overlay it describes, backed by the render-free core
  that file requires. This is a required part of the build.
- Ship the showcase `specs/showcase.md` describes, at the root of this
  repository.
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, speeds, and ranges in this specification are given in the
game's own logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the
right and `y` increases downward. Every rate is per second and every duration is
in seconds, integrated against the delta time each update is handed.

An entity's position is its centre. Wherever a tower or a surge unit is given,
taken, or reported as an `(x, y)` pair, that pair is the centre of the entity
rather than the top-left corner of its footprint or its sprite. The floor is a
grid of tiles, and the tile an entity occupies is the one containing its centre.
The tile-to-stage map and its inverse are in `specs/floor.md`, which owns them.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's work: the uniform scale that preserves the
  aspect ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The
  complete stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any
  pixel density, with the whole floor, the whole build panel, and all four edges
  visible and nothing clipped.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

## The screens the build has

The build carries eight screens, and the game is on exactly one of them at a
time: `title`, `modeselect`, `difficultyselect`, `howto`, `playing`, `paused`,
`victory`, and `gameover`. The `playing` screen runs in three sub-phases,
`opening`, `building`, and `wave`. `specs/screens.md` defines what each screen
shows and where each one leads, and `specs/waves.md` defines the phases.

## What the stage shows

While the game is being played, the floor carries the tile grid, every tower
standing on it at its own heat, every surge unit crossing it, the held build
preview and its range ring, and the range ring of a selected tower. The build
panel down the right-hand strip carries the readouts, the shop, the inspector,
and the wave controls. `specs/floor.md` gives the extent of each region and
`specs/hud.md` the readouts.

## Visual design

The reactor is dark and industrial, and the action is lit by heat. Choose the
colors and the typography, decide how the casing, the floor, a tower, a surge
unit, and the build panel are drawn, and decide what a heat ramp looks like.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Heat | An emitter's drawn color tracks its heat along a ramp, and the cold end and the near-redline end read plainly apart. |
| A tripped tower | A tripped tower is unmistakable and reads apart from an online tower at the same heat. |
| The heat read | Every tower carries a heat read on its footprint with a marker at its redline, so a tower sitting in its plateau is told from one that is cold or about to trip. |
| Radiator faces | A tower's radiator faces are drawn distinctly from its plain faces, so the player can see which sides shed heat. |
| Towers against the floor | A tower reads apart from the floor behind it. |
| The surge | Ground units, flyers, and the boss read apart from one another, from the floor, and from every color a tower shows anywhere on its heat ramp. |
| The casing and its openings | The casing reads as a solid wall enclosing the floor; a vent and an exhaust read apart from each other and from the casing, and an exhaust reads as dangerous. |
| The grid | The tile grid is visible on the floor at all times, including with no tower placed. |
| The build preview | A valid footprint and an invalid one read plainly apart, and the held preview and a selected tower draw a range ring. |
| The build zone | A mode that restricts building draws the zone it restricts building to. |
| Text | Every readout and every screen's text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The palette, the type, the glow, and every other aspect of the look are yours.

Every row of that table is about the FINISHED PICTURE, so each of them fixes a
step a player can see. Two colors read apart, and a thing is visible against what
is behind it, when they differ by at least `8` out of the `441` an RGB distance
spans — under two per cent of the scale, and the least a step can be and still be
a step rather than a shade. A grid line, a tower, a unit or a floor drawn under an
overlay at less than that is drawn where a player cannot see it, which the rows
above ask for the opposite of.

## Out of scope

- Network play, and any progress saved or persisted between sessions.
- Gamepad input. This build takes the pointer, touch, and the keyboard.
- A floor editor, a second floor, or a generated one. This build is the one fixed
  floor of `specs/floor.md`.
- A research or tech tree beyond the per-tower upgrades of `specs/building.md`.
